 "issue": 1650,
 "issue_title": "Bug: clarify HTML repr for non-empty zero-column frames",
 "issue_body": "## Summary\n`ArFrame._repr_html_()` renders non-empty zero-column frames as `(empty)`, even though the frame still has a positive row count.\n\n## Why it matters\nZero-column frames can happen after legitimate cleaning operations such as dropping all-empty columns. In notebooks, the HTML representation is the primary user-facing display. Showing `(empty)` makes it look like rows were removed, even when the C++ frame still reports preserved row count.\n\n## Evidence\n- `arnio/frame.py`: `_repr_html_()` returns `summary + \"<p><em>(empty)</em></p>\"` when `num_cols == 0 or num_rows == 0`.\n- `arnio/cleaning.py`: `drop_empty_columns()` can return `ArFrame(_Frame.from_dict({}, {}, frame.shape[0]), ...)`, preserving row count with zero columns.\n- Runtime check on current source plus the compiled extension shows `zero.shape == (2, 0)` but `_repr_html_()` renders `(empty)`.\n\n## Reproduction or trigger\n1. Create a frame with rows where every column is empty/null.\n2. Run `ar.drop_empty_columns(frame)`.\n3. Display the result in a notebook, or call `result._repr_html_()` directly.\n\n```python\nimport pandas as pd\nimport arnio as ar\n\nframe = ar.from_pandas(pd.DataFrame({\"a\": [None, None]}))\nzero = ar.drop_empty_columns(frame)\nprint(zero.shape)       # (2, 0)\nprint(zero._repr_html_())\n```\n\n## Expected behavior\nThe HTML output should distinguish zero rows from zero columns, for example by saying the frame has `2 rows \u00d7 0 cols` and no displayable columns.\n\n## Actual behavior\nThe summary preserves the shape, but the body renders `<em>(empty)</em>`, which implies there are no rows.\n\n## Suggested direction\nSplit the empty-frame branch into two cases:\n\n- `num_rows == 0`: render `(empty frame)`.\n- `num_cols == 0 and num_rows > 0`: render a clear message such as `(no columns to display; row count preserved)`.\n\nAdd a focused test for `_repr_html_()` on a zero-column frame with positive row count.\n\n## References\n- `arnio/frame.py`\n- `arnio/cleaning.py`\r\n",
 "issue_url": "https://github.com/im-anishraj/arnio/issues/1650",
 "pr": 2124,
 "pr_title": "fix: clarify HTML repr for non-empty zero-column frames",
 "pr_body": "Updated frame.py so `_repr_html_()` now treats zero rows and zero columns separately: zero rows still render \u201c(empty)\u201d, while non-empty zero-column frames render \u201c(no columns to display)\u201d. I also added a regression test in test_frame.py that builds a zero-column frame via `drop_empty_columns()` and checks that the row count is preserved in the HTML repr.\r\n\r\nValidation: `python -m py_compile frame.py tests/test_frame.py` passed. \r\n\r\ncloses #1650",
 "fix_commit": "2750075d6518056a0a05a7050341611c353d2400",
